    <!-- 11 MACHINE -->
    <section class="chapter mach" id="cluster" data-scene="nodes" data-chapter="The cluster">
      <div class="mach-sticky" id="mach-sticky">
        <div class="mach-canvas" id="mach-canvas"></div>
        <div class="mach-labels" id="mach-labels"></div>
        <div class="mach-tip panel" id="mach-tip"><b></b><span></span></div>
        <div class="mach-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Characteristic · distribution</p>
          <div class="mach-steps">
            <div class="mach-step on" data-ms="0">
              <h3>Sharding splits the data</h3>
              <p>A <strong>shard key</strong> is hashed and the hash decides which shard owns the record. Here the hash space 0–1023 is cut into three ranges, one per shard. Add shards and each one owns a smaller slice: that is horizontal scaling.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mach-step" data-ms="1">
              <h3>Replication copies it</h3>
              <p>Every shard keeps <strong>three copies</strong> (replication factor 3) on different nodes. Writes land on the primary and stream to replicas, so reads can be spread out and one dead disk loses nothing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mach-step" data-ms="2">
              <h3>Failover keeps it up</h3>
              <p>Click a glowing primary to kill it. The surviving replicas <strong>elect a new primary</strong> within seconds and writes continue. MongoDB works this way. Cassandra goes further and has no primary at all: any replica accepts writes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mach-hint">Drag to orbit · hover a node · click a node to fail or revive it</p>
        <div class="mach-ctl panel">
          <div class="panel-head"><span>Cluster console · 3 shards × RF 3</span><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span></div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <div class="row">
              <input class="field" id="m-key" value="user:42" aria-label="Key to write or read" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
              <button type="button" class="btn solid" id="m-write">Write</button>
              <button type="button" class="btn" id="m-read">Read</button>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <button type="button" class="btn" id="m-burst">Write 12 keys</button>
              <button type="button" class="btn" id="m-fail">Fail shard B primary</button>
              <button type="button" class="btn" id="m-cut">Exploded view</button>
              <button type="button" class="btn" id="m-reset">Reset</button>
            </div>
            <pre class="term mach-log" id="m-log"></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mach-fallback" id="mach-fallback">3D view needs WebGL. Turn on hardware acceleration and reload.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 CONSISTENCY -->
    <section class="chapter cons" id="consistency" data-scene="ring" data-chapter="Consistency">
      <div class="cons-head">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:20px">
          <p class="eyebrow">Characteristic · relaxed consistency</p>
          <h2 class="h-display" data-split>Three copies. <em>Who is right?</em></h2>
        </div>
        <p class="body-copy">With copies on several machines, a write takes time to reach all of them. Systems like Cassandra let you choose per request: <strong>W</strong> is how many replicas must confirm a write, <strong>R</strong> is how many you ask on a read. If <strong>R + W &gt; N</strong>, every read overlaps at least one replica that has the latest acknowledged write.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-grid">
        <article class="panel lab-ctl">
          <div class="panel-head"><span>Quorum controls</span><span>N = 3</span></div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <div class="stepper"><span class="lbl">Write acks <b>W</b></span><span class="ctl"><button type="button" data-st="w" data-d="-1" aria-label="Decrease W">−</button><output id="cw">1</output><button type="button" data-st="w" data-d="1" aria-label="Increase W">+</button></span></div>
            <div class="stepper"><span class="lbl">Read replicas <b>R</b></span><span class="ctl"><button type="button" data-st="r" data-d="-1" aria-label="Decrease R">−</button><output id="cr">1</output><button type="button" data-st="r" data-d="1" aria-label="Increase R">+</button></span></div>
            <div class="rule-box" id="rule-box"><span class="eq" id="rule-eq">1 + 1 = 2 ≤ 3</span><span id="rule-txt">Fast, but a read can miss the newest write.</span></div>
            <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" class="btn solid" id="c-write">Write new price</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="c-read">Read price</button></div>
            <p class="note">Each replica receives the write after a random network delay. Cut a replica off to simulate a network partition.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="panel lab-stage">
          <div class="panel-head"><span>Replicas of product 31 · price</span><span id="c-clock">t = 0 ms</span></div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <div class="replicas" id="replicas"></div>
            <div class="lab-result" id="c-result">Write a new price, then read it back before every replica has caught up.</div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="ab">
        <article class="panel"><div class="panel-body">
          <span class="tag sql">Relational default</span>
          <h3>ACID</h3>
          <ul><li><b>Atomic</b>, <b>consistent</b>, <b>isolated</b>, <b>durable</b> transactions.</li><li>Every read sees committed, valid data.</li><li>Coordination costs latency, especially across machines.</li></ul>
        </div></article>
        <article class="panel"><div class="panel-body">
          <span class="tag kv">Common NoSQL stance</span>
          <h3>BASE</h3>
          <ul><li><b>Basically available</b>: keep answering, even during failures.</li><li><b>Soft state</b>: replicas may briefly differ.</li><li><b>Eventually consistent</b>: with no new writes, all copies converge.</li></ul>
        </div></article>
        <article class="panel"><div class="panel-body">
          <span class="tag">Team 10 goes deeper</span>
          <h3>CAP</h3>
          <ul><li>Brewer’s conjecture (2000), proved by Gilbert and Lynch (2002).</li><li>When the network <b>partitions</b>, a distributed store must choose: refuse requests to stay <b>consistent</b>, or keep serving and stay <b>available</b>.</li><li>Try it above: cut a replica off with W = 3.</li></ul>
        </div></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 13 COMPARE -->
    <section class="chapter cmp" id="compare" data-scene="grid" data-chapter="SQL vs NoSQL">
      <div class="cmp-head">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:20px;max-width:760px">
          <p class="eyebrow">Side by side</p>
          <h2 class="h-display" data-split>SQL vs <em>NoSQL</em></h2>
          <p class="body-copy">Different bets about what matters most. The textbook contrast holds, but modern systems keep borrowing from each other.</p>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn" id="cmp-blur" aria-pressed="false">Show where the line blurs</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tbl-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>SQL · relational</th><th>NoSQL · non-relational</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Data model</th><td>Tables of rows and columns</td><td>Key-value, document, wide-column or graph</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Schema</th><td>Fixed, defined before writing (schema on write)</td><td>Flexible, interpreted when reading (schema on read)<span class="blur">Most document stores support optional schema validation.</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th>Relationships</th><td>Foreign keys and joins</td><td>Embedded data, or links resolved by the application<span class="blur">Graph databases make relationships their strongest feature.</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th>Scaling</th><td>Mostly vertical; sharding is possible but hard</td><td>Horizontal by design<span class="blur">Distributed SQL systems such as Google Spanner and CockroachDB scale out too.</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th>Transactions</th><td>ACID across many rows and tables</td><td>Often per record or per partition, tunable consistency<span class="blur">MongoDB has supported multi-document ACID transactions since version 4.0 (2018).</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th>Query language</th><td>SQL, an ISO standard</td><td>Product-specific APIs and languages<span class="blur">Cassandra has CQL, Couchbase has SQL++; PostgreSQL stores and indexes JSON with JSONB.</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th>Best at</th><td>Complex queries, reporting, strict integrity</td><td>Huge scale, high write rates, evolving or varied data</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Examples</th><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server</td><td>MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra, Neo4j, DynamoDB</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="marq" data-dir="1" aria-hidden="true"><div class="marq-in"><span><b>Part 03</b>Advantages</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 03</b>Advantages</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 03</b>Advantages</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 03</b>Advantages</span><i>✦</i></div></div>
    <!-- 14 ADVANTAGES -->
    <section class="chapter adv" id="advantages" data-scene="nodes" data-chapter="Advantages">
      <div class="adv-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Advantages</p>
        <h2 class="h-display" data-split>What you <em>gain.</em></h2>
        <p class="body-copy">Six reasons teams reach for NoSQL. Keep scrolling: the cards stack.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack" id="stack">
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · scalability</p><h3>Grows sideways</h3><p class="body-copy">Need more capacity? Add nodes. The cluster rebalances data onto them while it keeps serving. Capacity grows roughly in step with the hardware you add.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> data is partitioned by key from the start.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-scale" aria-hidden="true"><i style="left:8%"></i><i style="left:21%;animation-delay:.4s"></i><i style="left:34%;animation-delay:.8s"></i><i style="left:47%;animation-delay:1.2s"></i><i style="left:60%;animation-delay:1.6s"></i><i style="left:73%;animation-delay:2s"></i><i style="left:8%;bottom:48%;animation-delay:2.4s"></i><i style="left:21%;bottom:48%;animation-delay:2.8s"></i><i style="left:34%;bottom:48%;animation-delay:3.2s"></i><span class="vis-cap">nodes joining a live cluster</span></div>
        </article>
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · flexibility</p><h3>Schema that keeps up</h3><p class="body-copy">Ship a feature that needs a new field today, without a migration window. Old and new shapes live side by side in the same collection.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> structure is checked by the application, or by optional validation.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-flex" aria-hidden="true"><div class="dshape">{'{'}<span class="f" style="animation-delay:0s">  "name": "Asha",</span><span class="f" style="animation-delay:.6s">  "email": "asha@mail.in",</span><span class="f" style="animation-delay:1.2s">  "phone": "98xxxxxx12",</span><span class="f" style="animation-delay:1.8s">  "tags": ["vip", "beta"],</span><span class="f" style="animation-delay:2.4s">  "loyalty_tier": "gold"</span>{'}'}</div><span class="vis-cap">fields arrive as features ship</span></div>
        </article>
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · performance</p><h3>Reads in one hop</h3><p class="body-copy">Data that is read together is stored together. A profile page becomes one key lookup or one document fetch instead of a five-table join.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> you model data around your queries.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-perf" aria-hidden="true"><span class="lab" style="top:22%">one document read</span><div class="lane" style="top:34%"><i></i></div><span class="lab" style="top:56%">five-table join (illustrative)</span><div class="lane slow" style="top:68%"><i></i></div><span class="vis-cap">relative, not measured</span></div>
        </article>
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · availability</p><h3>Survives failure</h3><p class="body-copy">With copies on several nodes, and often several data centres, a crashed machine is routine. Replicas take over and clients barely notice.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> replication and automatic failover are built in.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-avail" aria-hidden="true"><div class="n p" style="left:8%;top:30%">primary</div><div class="n r" style="left:39%;top:30%">replica</div><div class="n" style="left:70%;top:30%">replica</div><span class="vis-cap">primary fails · replica promoted</span></div>
        </article>
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · cost</p><h3>Commodity hardware</h3><p class="body-copy">Clusters of ordinary servers, or managed cloud services billed by use, replace one expensive high-end machine. You pay for capacity as you need it.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> the software handles failures the hardware does not prevent.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-cost" aria-hidden="true"><div class="bars"><i style="height:30%"></i><i style="height:42%;animation-delay:.3s"></i><i style="height:55%;animation-delay:.6s"></i><i style="height:66%;animation-delay:.9s"></i><i style="height:78%;animation-delay:1.2s"></i></div><span class="vis-cap">capacity added in small steps</span></div>
        </article>
        <article class="scard">
          <div><p class="k">Advantage · developer speed</p><h3>Matches your code</h3><p class="body-copy">A JSON document looks like the object in your program. Less mapping code, fewer schema meetings, faster iteration in agile teams.</p><div class="why"><span><b>Because:</b> the stored shape mirrors the in-memory shape.</span></div></div>
          <div class="vis vis-dev" aria-hidden="true"><div class="code" id="dev-code"></div><span class="vis-cap">object in, document out</span></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="marq" data-dir="-1" aria-hidden="true"><div class="marq-in"><span><b>Part 04</b>Limitations</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 04</b>Limitations</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 04</b>Limitations</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 04</b>Limitations</span><i>✦</i></div></div>
    <!-- 15 LIMITATIONS -->
    <section class="chapter lim" id="limitations" data-scene="strand" data-chapter="Limitations">
      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px;max-width:900px">
        <p class="eyebrow">Limitations</p>
        <h2 class="h-display" data-split>What it <em>costs.</em></h2>
        <p class="body-copy">Every advantage on the previous cards was paid for somewhere. Hover or tap a card to see how teams cope.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lim-grid">
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>Instant consistency</h3><p>Replicas can disagree for a moment. A read may return yesterday’s price.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Tuning per query</h3><p>Use quorum reads and writes, or strongly consistent reads, where correctness matters, and eventual reads elsewhere.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>Easy joins</h3><p>Most NoSQL stores cannot join collections efficiently. Ad hoc questions get hard.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Modelling for queries</h3><p>Embed related data, keep one table per query pattern, or export to an analytics warehouse.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>One standard</h3><p>Every product has its own API and query language. Skills do not transfer cleanly.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Standardising</h3><p>Pick one or two stores per company, and use SQL-like layers such as CQL or SQL++ where they exist.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>Single source of truth</h3><p>Denormalised data copies facts into many places. Updates can miss a copy.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Owning updates</h3><p>Change data through one service, propagate with events, and run reconciliation jobs.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>Multi-record transactions</h3><p>Many stores only guarantee atomic changes to one record or one partition.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Choosing carefully</h3><p>Keep money and inventory in a relational or transactional store. Some NoSQL systems now offer transactions too.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
        <button type="button" class="flip"><div class="flip-in"><div class="face front"><span class="k">You lose</span><div><h3>Decades of tooling</h3><p>Reporting, BI and admin tools grew up around SQL. Expertise is scarcer.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip</span></div><div class="face back"><span class="k">Teams cope by</span><div><h3>Using managed services</h3><p>Let the cloud provider run backups and scaling, and check driver and monitoring support before adopting.</p></div><span class="hint">Tap to flip back</span></div></div></button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="marq" data-dir="1" aria-hidden="true"><div class="marq-in"><span><b>Part 05</b>Applications</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 05</b>Applications</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 05</b>Applications</span><i>✦</i><span><b>Part 05</b>Applications</span><i>✦</i></div></div>
