    <!-- 19 RECAP -->
    <section class="chapter recap" id="recap" data-scene="nebula" data-chapter="Recap">
      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px;max-width:900px">
        <p class="eyebrow">Recap</p>
        <h2 class="h-display" data-split>Five things to <em>keep.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="recap-rows">
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">01</span><h3>Need</h3><p>Volume, velocity and variety outgrew one relational server. Scaling up hits a cost curve and a ceiling.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">02</span><h3>Characteristics</h3><p>Non-relational models, flexible schema, horizontal scaling through sharding, replication with failover, and tunable or eventual consistency.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">03</span><h3>Advantages</h3><p>Scalability, flexibility, fast reads shaped by queries, high availability, commodity cost and developer speed.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">04</span><h3>Limitations</h3><p>Weaker consistency, limited joins, no single standard, duplicated data, narrower transactions and younger tooling.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">05</span><h3>Applications</h3><p>Feeds, carts, catalogues, telemetry, analytics, gaming, recommendations and caching, usually next to a relational database.</p></div>
      </div>
